WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.480
I removed the core combo from my bracket 4 pirate&nbsp;
deck, along with some of the supporting cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.000
for it, replacing them with some more generalist&nbsp;
cards along with a couple bizarre finishers. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.280
it wasn’t exactly a replacement. The original deck&nbsp;
is still fully playable, but I made a 10-card swap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.200
out package that could be implemented to move the&nbsp;
deck down from a cutthroat high power combo deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.160
that usually wins on turn 5 to a grindier pirate&nbsp;
midrange deck that usually wins on more like turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.440
8-10. On the surface that sounds fine, great&nbsp;
even—I have a deck whose core I like, and I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.600
to split it into a high-powered bracket 4 style&nbsp;
version and a more mid-powered bracket 3 style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.920
version. However, the resulting theoretically&nbsp;
mid-power deck feels very strange to play, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.160
I think it points to some odd truths about what it&nbsp;
means for a deck to be a particular power level.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.840
Something that might have already caught&nbsp;
your attention is that 10-card number—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.720
firmly bracket 4 deck is really just 10 cards&nbsp;
from being a totally fair bracket 3 deck?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.680
And this wasn’t swapping 10 good cards for 10 bad&nbsp;
cards either—this is more like swapping 1 insanely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.560
good card and 9 highly specific support&nbsp;
cards for 10 generally decent cards. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.880
how does that work? Well, to get into that,&nbsp;
I need to start by covering what the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.400
deck does. This deck was intended to be the most&nbsp;
powerful possible pirates deck I could make on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.920
$100 budget, and it does so by leveraging&nbsp;
its command zone extremely aggressively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.600
Francisco is a two mana flying pirate that&nbsp;
filters whenever one or more of my pirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.000
hit an opponent, and Malcolm is a three mana&nbsp;
flying pirate that makes a treasure whenever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.560
one or more of my pirates hit an opponent, so the&nbsp;
core of this deck ended up being these two paired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.360
with a one mana pirate to get the deck set up&nbsp;
with a powerful early engine. This engine builds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.160
into a combo turn with Time Sieve, a two mana&nbsp;
artifact that can sacrifice five artifacts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.440
grant an extra turn. Malcolm creates 3 artifacts&nbsp;
per turn as long as I’m hitting three players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.080
so all that’s required to keep taking turns with&nbsp;
Time Sieve is a card like Academy Manufactor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.560
Irenicus’s Vile Duplication, or Storm Fleet&nbsp;
Negotiator that can close that gap with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.400
extra artifacts per turn. Paired with&nbsp;
a bunch of tutors and draw engines to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.720
find the various pieces—and an eye watering&nbsp;
13 counterspells to keep my plans safe with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.680
spare treasures—this deck can pretty reliably&nbsp;
yield well-protected combo wins on turn 5-6.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.280
So this is a pretty strong deck, but it’s also&nbsp;
not one I’ve ended up playing with a whole lot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.080
I love playing a highly interactive deck with lots&nbsp;
of evasive creatures and coastal piracy effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.760
but this deck was that plus a fast combo win,&nbsp;
so it just doesn’t feel right in most games.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.480
And fundamentally this is why I was interested in&nbsp;
creating a pivot for this deck—to let my pirate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.280
guys see more games. The budget of this deck&nbsp;
also might make it more clear why I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.120
10 cards would be a fine number to swap out—the&nbsp;
card quality here just isn’t that high. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.320
no game-changers in this original deck, and not&nbsp;
a whole lot of what you might consider staples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.560
If we look at the 13 counterspells in particular,&nbsp;
there are a lot of weird ones you might not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.640
heard of: Spell Snare, Lookout’s Dispersal,&nbsp;
Memory Lapse. I also run every one-mana pirate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.520
in the game, including the vanilla heavy hitter&nbsp;
Grasping Scoundrel and the Foglio classic that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.520
is Cloud Pirates. So surely, I figured, this would&nbsp;
be an easy power-down. My deck is full of dogshit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.320
so I’ll just cut the Time Sieve, some tutors, and&nbsp;
some of the hyper-low-cost interaction spells. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.040
their place will come in a few pieces of creature&nbsp;
removal, a few value engines, another land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.560
and a few much goofier wincons. One of these&nbsp;
wincons is Vorpal Sword, an equipment that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.760
turn an evasive creature and eleven total mana&nbsp;
into a dead opponent in short order. The other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.160
two are the commander storm cards Skull Storm and&nbsp;
Echo Storm, which help mitigate the weakness of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.120
this deck getting its commanders shut down a lot.&nbsp;
All three of these are pieces of tech that I tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.040
out in the high-power version of the deck, but&nbsp;
which just didn’t really make sense in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.600
power level, and it’s satisfying to be able to&nbsp;
still let them live in a version of the deck.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.240
So here was the deck, now split into two.&nbsp;
I double-sleeved the whole deck in Dragon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.800
Shield outer sleeves, and then color-coded the&nbsp;
two 10 card swap packages with ordinary colored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.960
sleeves. If you want to attempt something similar&nbsp;
with these outer sleeves, follow my affiliate link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.080
in the description and use code “salubrious” at&nbsp;
checkout. That aside, how did this new version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:10.080
of the deck feel? Was it indeed a mid-power&nbsp;
deck? Well, I can’t say the answer to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.440
is no. It has won a pretty unremarkable number&nbsp;
of games, and it threatens wins on a timeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.200
that’s pretty normal for a bracket 3 deck—usually&nbsp;
around turn 8-9. The overall plan for the deck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.600
evasive creature beatdown, with a lot of effects&nbsp;
that generate value when combat damage is dealt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.840
and the deck’s more dedicated wincons&nbsp;
dovetail naturally from this commander-centric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:35.120
light-pirate-beats plan. However, there is still a&nbsp;
distinctly weird character to this deck, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.400
comes from how precision-built it is to be strong&nbsp;
early in the game, and how much protection it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.920
for its gameplan. If it does indeed land turn 1&nbsp;
pirate, turn 2 Francisco, turn 3 Malcolm, or an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:51.600
equivalently strong rollout, it starts building up&nbsp;
resources really fast. Three treasures a turn is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.760
pretty standard, and with one of the seven Coastal&nbsp;
Piracy effects in this version of the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:01.200
it will also often be drawing three cards a turn&nbsp;
starting on turn 4, with that number often scaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.240
up quite a bit within a couple turns. The deck&nbsp;
does exactly what you would want a deck to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.280
as it’s gearing up for a crushing turn five or&nbsp;
six win, because that’s what the core of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.360
deck was designed to do, but in this version&nbsp;
that crushing win never quite materializes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.360
The deck just keeps drawing cards and making&nbsp;
treasures and playing pirates until either it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.720
finds one of its finishers, wins through critical&nbsp;
mass, or loses its footing and falls behind.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.560
This effect is amplified by this deck’s&nbsp;
interaction suite. The original deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.640
had 13 counterspells and a pirate that&nbsp;
can be sacrificed to protect something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.160
as well as seven removal spells. This new version&nbsp;
effectively trades three one mana counterspells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:46.320
for a set of creature removal tools that cost&nbsp;
3, 4, and 9 mana respectively. In terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.280
this deck’s ability to slow opponents, this feels&nbsp;
pretty fine—17 of the deck’s 20 interactive tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.160
are single-target in nature, and half of the&nbsp;
deck’s counterspells can’t even hit creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.720
This deck can draw a lot of cards and create a&nbsp;
decent number of treasures, but it’s just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.440
capable of keeping the whole table suppressed&nbsp;
for any significant length of time. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.720
I’ve had several games where this deck did seem&nbsp;
to give off “controlling pubstomper” energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.600
and that fact fundamentally comes down to the&nbsp;
protection suite here. This deck is carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.240
engineered to be as good at protecting its board&nbsp;
as possible, right from the first Malcolm trigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:26.160
on turn 3. In the original version there were&nbsp;
14 protective tools, and here that number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:32.240
drops to 11, but in a deck with plentiful draw and&nbsp;
filtering that still ends up feeling plenty large.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.520
And, this feature intersects with the deck’s early&nbsp;
power spike in some really interesting ways. If on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.680
turn 4 I have three pirates, am creating three&nbsp;
treasures per turn, and am drawing three or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.960
more cards per turn, that generally makes me the&nbsp;
early big bad of a bracket 3 commander game. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.440
when the removal inevitably starts flying,&nbsp;
I get to effortlessly brush it aside with my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.240
plentiful treasure mana even if I tapped out, and&nbsp;
my draw engines will typically draw me into more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.560
counterspells and additional draw engines, which&nbsp;
will in turn draw me into even more protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.680
for my commanders. In the most extreme games,&nbsp;
I’ve thrown out five or more counterspells to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.320
protect my board in the first six turns of the&nbsp;
game, all while drawing piles upon piles of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.800
through a wide board of pirates and multiple&nbsp;
coastal piracy effects. And just think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.600
how this looks for my opponents: I’m the deck&nbsp;
that’s drawing 8 cards per turn with my board of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.720
many permanents and countering removal spell after&nbsp;
removal spell, seemingly executing an unstoppable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.000
gameplan with cold precision. Every attempt&nbsp;
to slow me down falls short, every point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.080
resistance anticipated. I’d call this pubstomping,&nbsp;
but it’s more like pubstomp-simulator because I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:41.520
not actually dealing a lot of damage or removing&nbsp;
a lot of opposing threats, I’m just doing all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.160
other things that a pubstomping player would do.&nbsp;
This is made all the worse because when this deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.240
does lose its foothold, it’s not usually&nbsp;
because my opponents got off a sick alpha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.960
strike spell or landed a perfectly timed board&nbsp;
wipe—the first couple of those got countered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.960
Instead, the deck falls behind with a whimper,&nbsp;
taking damage and losing permanents here or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.640
there until it’s forced to allow a board&nbsp;
wipe to resolve, or alternatively forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:08.560
to offer up its board as blocks in the face of an&nbsp;
overwhelming attack. It does succumb to pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.760
but it feels more like collapsing under its own&nbsp;
weight than getting set back by an opponent.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.920
In a dedicated playgroup, I think basically&nbsp;
all of this deck’s weirdness would melt away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.880
after playing against it a handful of times. If&nbsp;
you know the deck’s formula—early power spike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.880
glut of single target interaction, commander&nbsp;
storm cards to give a second wind after an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.080
eventual fall-off—in that case, none of this&nbsp;
feels all that scary anymore. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.760
the people at the LGS seeing this for the first&nbsp;
time, and maybe even meeting me for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.680
time? They’re interpreting opposing decks through&nbsp;
the lens of the decks they’ve already played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.400
against, and based on the idiocultures of the&nbsp;
Magic: the Gathering spaces they inhabit. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.880
this feature can go the opposite way as well.&nbsp;
Just as me drawing half a dozen cards per turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:53.440
doesn’t necessarily signal being unstoppably far&nbsp;
ahead in an early game centered deck like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.960
seeing somebody doing entirely mundane things in&nbsp;
a late-game centered deck could easily be quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:05.200
problematic. In my Sharuum control deck, I’ve&nbsp;
noticed that if I’m not clearly behind on turn 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.440
I’m actually ahead. If my life total is still&nbsp;
in the 30s, my hand is decently stocked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:14.480
and my board isn’t utterly dwarfed by that of my&nbsp;
opponents, I probably have at least a 40% chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:19.360
of winning the game, and that just comes down to&nbsp;
the deck being engineered to utterly dominate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.920
the late game. I do try to communicate this&nbsp;
power profile, because I want a fair game:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.440
“Hey folks, this is my Sharuum control deck,&nbsp;
I sure hope I make it to the late game and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:34.000
to do awesome stuff with Astral Slide.” I’ll&nbsp;
likewise do a bit of that with the bracket 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.720
version of the pirates deck, communicating that&nbsp;
my two most powerful wincons are Vorpal Sword&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.680
and Haunted One, but with both of these&nbsp;
decks communication can only go so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.960
and that comes down to yet another challenge&nbsp;
of commander with randos: the complexity.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.440
With a playgroup you can get to know all of your&nbsp;
opponents’ decks in and out and have an intuitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:58.640
feel for when a deck is or isn’t having a good&nbsp;
game, but with fresh opponents and less familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:03.840
decks that can be much more challenging. Even&nbsp;
if somebody tells you the rough profile of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:10.800
deck, that’s something you need to remember,&nbsp;
and their account might omit details or include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.840
built-in assumptions that you don’t necessarily&nbsp;
share. So, people go based on what they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.680
see—life total, cards in hand, and especially&nbsp;
what’s on board. If I’m there with a wide board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:26.720
of pirates resolving 12 combat damage triggers per&nbsp;
turn, it’s easy for the person quietly ramping or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:31.440
tutoring or setting up synergy pieces to fall&nbsp;
into the background. Likewise, that background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:35.680
person is me when I’m playing one of my greedier&nbsp;
decks. Fundamentally, the biggest thing this comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:40.720
down to is how telegraphed a deck is in what it’s&nbsp;
doing. A deck that’s beating the “I’m a problem”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.200
drum starting on turn 4 with a flurry of activity&nbsp;
on board is going to be much more noticed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:50.560
and more easily kept under control, than a deck&nbsp;
whose resources are more subtle. A deck that wins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:55.360
highly visibly on turn 6 could easily feel much&nbsp;
more fair to a table of strangers than a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:01.440
who pulls a combo win out of nowhere on turn&nbsp;
7, even if it’s theoretically a stronger deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:05.840
This all could sound like a “problem” of some&nbsp;
sort, but to me it’s ultimately more of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:10.480
interesting observation. Yes, tables with less&nbsp;
familiar players are going to be different than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:15.440
tables with a playgroup, and those distinctions&nbsp;
are interesting to dive into, but in neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:21.280
place is this pubstomping-simulator pirates deck&nbsp;
truly a problem. It feels like it swims against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:25.920
the social forces of playing commander with&nbsp;
strangers, but so does any deck that dares stray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.720
from the most conventional molds of commander&nbsp;
decks. And even if my spidey senses feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.280
they’re picking up on some social friction when&nbsp;
I’m hitting my strongest turns with this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.240
ultimately I don’t think most people are&nbsp;
walking away from playing against this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:44.480
deck having their day made worse for it. They’re&nbsp;
just playing commander, just like anybody else.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:49.040
Thanks for watching, and thanks to Archidekt for&nbsp;
supporting the channel. Archidekt’s maybeboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:52.880
categories give me an elegant way to do&nbsp;
something weird like this with a deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.040
and if you want to take a look at this deck’s&nbsp;
list you can find that down in the description.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.760
I’d also like to thank my supporters on YouTube&nbsp;
and Patreon, some of whom are on-screen now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:05.440
If you want to support me in making this sort&nbsp;
of content, as well as disappearing to Serbia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:09.075
occasionally, you can join my patreon or&nbsp;
sign up for a YouTube membership today.

